Title: Raise the resident rules budget to 36500 to fund the waiter rule
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-09-10T07:57Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

`tests/test_rules_resident_budget.py` caps the resident rule set at 36 000
characters — the tax every session of every project pays before the first
question. Its docstring makes raising the cap a deliberate act: "a decision to
pay more on every session, in every project, forever: argue it in a ticket, as
`.ruff.toml` suppressions are argued". This is that argument.

## What needs the room

A resident rule in `rules/workflow.md` forbidding shell loops that poll for a
process. The defect: a `pgrep` predicate matches the waiting shell's own command
line, so the loop waits for itself; it also outlives the session, reparented to
init, polling forever with nothing to sweep it.

The measurement, from 551 session transcripts over 101 days:

- **105 waiters written, every one by an agent, none by the author.** Several
  show an agent that had already met the self-match and was working around it
  (`pgrep -c … -le 1`, a double negation) — the defect is the shape, not the
  predicate.
- **Two stranded processes alive in 43 hours of uptime**, at 14 h 57 and
  16 h 49. Both found by accident during an unrelated cleanup.

The lesson has been in memory (`feedback_pgrep_waiter_matches_itself`) since
2026-09-09, with the correct recipe. Memory is recalled by relevance, so it
reached none of the 105. Resident rule bodies are in front of every session.
That difference is the whole reason this needs the budget rather than a note.

## Why not the alternatives

- **Scope it with `paths:`** — does not work. `paths:` fires on the file being
  edited; this rule must fire when an agent is about to write a Bash command.
- **Write it shorter** — the minimum viable form (prohibition, mechanism,
  alternative) measures ~265 characters against 74 available. The shipped text
  is 474 after two tightening passes.
- **Trim elsewhere to fund it** — the 2026-09-09 trimming pass already removed
  the incident parentheticals from the resident bodies; what remains is rules.
  Cutting into it to fund this would arbitrate against that pass. Deferred
  deliberately, not skipped: see Exit criteria.
- **Leave it in memory** — the status quo, and its result is known: the note
  existed while all 105 were written.

## The headroom was already spent

The cap was not reached by this change. PR #853 added ~400 characters to
`rules/claude-code.md` the previous day, taking the set from ~35 500 to 35 926
and leaving 74. The budget allocates first-come with no reservation, so the
gate fires on whoever arrives next rather than on whoever grew the set. Worth
knowing when reading this raise as evidence of drift: it is one gate firing
twice in two days, and the second firing is this ticket.

## Decision

Raise `RESIDENT_BUDGET` from 36 000 to 36 500, a loan of 500 characters
(~125 tokens per session, +1.4%), **against a trimming pass** the author has
scheduled for the day after this filing (2026-09-11). The raise is not the end
state; it buys the rule its place until the trim repays it.

## Relevant files

- `tests/test_rules_resident_budget.py` — the cap and its two ratchets
- `rules/workflow.md` — where the rule lands, under Micro-turn discipline
- `projects/-home-haduong--claude/memory/feedback_pgrep_waiter_matches_itself.md`
  — the memory note the rule promotes

## Invariants

- The other three ratchets in that test keep passing: every resident file
  declared in the index, no shell recipes in a resident body, no ticket numbers
  in one.
- The rule text carries no ticket reference, no shell fence.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `RESIDENT_BUDGET` is 36 500, with the loan and its repayment recorded
- [ ] The waiter rule is in `rules/workflow.md` and the whole budget test passes
- [ ] The memory note records that the detector was built and dropped, and
      carries the reparentation signal for whoever builds the sweep later
- [ ] A trimming pass repays the 500 and lowers the cap again — a follow-up
      ticket, filed when that pass is scheduled, not blocked on here
